#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
xsync: small rsync wrapper for local-to-remote project sync.
Reads .xsync.toml from the project root (current dir or any parent).

The remote is normally an SSH target, "host:/path/to/project/", where host is
an alias from ~/.ssh/config (key auth, ideally with ControlMaster so repeated
syncs reuse one connection). A local path (e.g. a mounted volume) also works.

Usage:
    xsync                      # open the session: compare, then sync or watch with single keys
    xsync --sync               # sync the entire project once and exit
    xsync path/to/file.py      # sync a single file or folder
    xsync --dry-run            # show what would be synced, no transfer
    xsync file.py --dry-run    # dry-run for a single file or folder
    xsync --watch              # open the session with watch mode already on
    xsync --watch --interval 5 # watch with a 5-second debounce window
    xsync --init               # create a starter .xsync.toml in current dir
    xsync --status             # show the resolved setup and check the host; no sync
    xsync --verbose            # show rsync's raw output instead of the summary
    xsync --version            # show the version
    xsync --help               # show this help

Keys in the session: s sync, w watch on/off, c compare, f sync a file or folder,
r reconnect, i info (the setup, as --status), e errors, q quit. Without a terminal (piped, scripted), plain
`xsync` syncs once and exits.
"""

